TI  - Use of n-3 fatty acid-containing lipid emulsions in the Intensive Care Unit environment: the clinician's view
T2  - CLINICAL NUTRITION
AB  - Recent advances in clinical nutrition include the combination of caloric support and modulation of the immune system ('immunonutrition'). For a long time, dietary n-3 lipids have been used for their potential to reduce the activity of inflammatory diseases. In an Intensive Care Unit (ICU) setting there is often a need for parenteral nutrition in patients with acute inflammatory diseases such as sepsis and adult respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). The underlying mechanism of these diseases is a hyperinflammatory response with exaggerated leukocyte activity, consecutive microcirculatory disorders and secondary organ damage. The therapy of these patients is hampered by the fact that hyperinflammatory [systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS)] and hypoinflammatory phases [compensatory anti-response syndrome (CARS)] can alternate during the disease progress. For that reason a broad spectrum of anti-inflammatory agents has failed to improve mortality in septic patients: while effectively antagonising the hyperinflammatory response during SIRS they further reduce the antimicrobial defence capacity during CARS. An anti-inflammatory cytokine that is beneficial during early sepsis may be deleterious when given during late sepsis. Recently, n-3 lipid emulsions became available as a new approach combining nutrition and pharmacological effects. Dietary n-3 fatty acids may not be suitable under septic conditions to ameliorate inflammation as months are needed for a maximum effect. In contrast, parenteral infusions of synthetic lipids markedly overcome kinetics and extent of dietary substitution. Using n-3 lipids for parenteral nutrition, hyperinflammatory events in the course of sepsis may be mitigated and the consecutive anti-inflammatory phase may be avoidable. As n-3 fatty acids interfere with transmembrane signalling events they seem to be effective in reducing hyper-reactivity in both extremes. Due to this feature n-3 lipids inhibit the uncontrolled release of the defensive leukocyte arsenal ('wasted leukocyte response') and redirect the host-defence against invading microbes. (C) 2002, Elsevier Science Ltd. All rights reserved.
