TI  - EFFECTS OF VARIOUS SERUM CHOLESTEROL LOWERING PROCEDURES AND AGENTS IN PATIENTS WITH CORONARY ARTERY DISEASE
T2  - TEXAS REPORTS ON BIOLOGY AND MEDICINE
AB  - In a series of over 500 cardiac patients, those with clinical evidence of aortitis and coronary artery disease had, as a rule, high serum cholesterol levels. These patients were treated with various regimens for varying lengths of time. Most patients on long-term dietary restriction of fats as out-patients responded with an average reduction of 12.5% in cholesterol, 9% in phospholipids and 5% in beta lipoproteins; this therapy was administered for 1,100 days. The administration of phytosterols and estrogens and nonfeminizing chlorotrianisine showed very little more reduction, 13% and 14%. Phenyethyl acetadmide, PEAA., alone reduced cholesterol levels only 9%; when it was combined with either niacin, 3 x 50 mg, or MRD 507A, 6 x 500 mg, reductions were 20% and 8.6%, respectively. Cholestyramine, 4 g tid, accomplished about the same amount of reduction. On the other hand, the addition of capsules of unsaturated fatty acid to the ordinary diet was followed by a 4% reduction. Triparanol (MER-29) produced only 10.2% reduction of cholesterol levels in 103 patients over 480 days; the serious side effects of this drug caused it to be withdrawn from the market. The administration of the first dextrarotary thyroid homologue, D-di-iodothyronine acetic acid (Diac), resulted in only 10.5% lowering of cholesterol, 17.5% of phospholipids and 2.5% of beta lipoproteins. However, D-tri-iodothyronine propionic acid, (Triopron) produced an average reduction of 37.5% in cholesterol levels in 60 patients over an average of 290 days, while the D-tetra-iodothyronine, sodium D-thyroxin (Choloxin), caused a reduction of 21-5% in 61 patients over an average period of 271 days. These latter data, especially the conspicuous effects of thyroid homologues, indicate that these are effective agents for the reduction of human serum cholesterol, phospholipid and beta lipoprotein levels. The patients on the thyroid homologues therapy volunteered that they felt better and had lost some weight. Side effects of thyroid homologues were minimal, consisting of nervousness, tachycardia, and premature contractions, with the occasional production of angina pectoris, but most of the patients improved on this therapy.
