TI  - SOME EFFECTS OF GAMMA-LINOLENIC ACID ON CULTURED HUMAN ESOPHAGEAL-CARCINOMA CELLS
T2  - SOUTH AFRICAN MEDICAL JOURNAL
AB  - According to Horrobin''s hypothesis, malignant cells may be dependent upon the deficiency of .gamma.-linoleic acid, a metabolic intermediate in the biosynthesis of prostaglandin E1. Cells of 2 continuous human esophageal carcinoma lines [HCU 18, HCU 39] were treated by the addition of .gamma.-linolenic acid (GLA) at concentrations of 10-60 .mu.g/ml culture medium. After 7 days of exposure to GLA, pronounced morphological changes became evident and culminated in cell death. GLA-mediated effects were time- and dose-dependent and varied slightly with the degree of histological differentiation of the lines tested. The findings may have clinical implications.
